1. What is a Deployment Rollout?

A rollout in Kubernetes manages the lifecycle of your application updates. When you update a Deployment (e.g., changing container image versions, environment variables, or resource limits), Kubernetes performs a rolling update. This ensures zero downtime by gradually terminating old pods and spinning up new pods with the updated version.

2. Checking Rollout Status (kubectl rollout status)

Whenever you trigger an update or scale a deployment, you can monitor its progress in real-time using:

Bash

kubectl rollout status deployment/<deployment-name>
Purpose: It blocks and waits until the rollout operation completes successfully (all new pods are up and running, and old pods are safely terminated).

Common Output: deployment "nginxdeploy" successfully rolled out

3. Viewing Rollout History (kubectl rollout history)

Kubernetes keeps track of revision history so you can see past changes or roll back if something goes wrong.

View all revisions:

Bash
kubectl rollout history deployment/<deployment-name>
View details of a specific revision:

Bash
kubectl rollout history deployment/<deployment-name> --revision=2

4. Rolling Back an Update (kubectl rollout undo)

If a new update introduces a bug or causes crashes, you can instantly revert your deployment to the previous version or a specific revision.

Undo to the immediate previous version:

Bash
kubectl rollout undo deployment/<deployment-name>
Roll back to a specific revision:

Bash
kubectl rollout undo deployment/<deployment-name> --to-revision=1

5. Managing Rollouts (Pause & Resume)

Sometimes you want to make multiple changes to a deployment configuration (like changing images and environment variables simultaneously) without triggering a rollout for every single change.

Pause a rollout:

Bash
kubectl rollout pause deployment/<deployment-name>
Resume a rollout:

Bash
kubectl rollout resume deployment/<deployment-name>

6. Key Best Practices for Production

Set revisionHistoryLimit: By default, Kubernetes stores 10 revisions. You can configure this in your deployment spec to save cluster storage.

Use Probes (livenessProbe and readinessProbe): Ensure Kubernetes knows when a new pod is truly ready to accept traffic before cutting off the old pods.